WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.860
FedRAMP High is connected to NIST security control concepts and applies them in a cloud

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:06.315
authorization context.

00:00:06.315 --> 00:00:10.040
Control families cover areas such as access control, audit and accountability,

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:15.085
configuration management, contingency planning, identification and authentication,

00:00:15.085 --> 00:00:18.810
incident response, risk assessment, system and communications protection,

00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:19.915
and related domains.

00:00:19.915 --> 00:00:23.590
High-impact use cases demand that those expectations be translated into mature

00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:25.680
implementation and evidence.

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:28.760
Control implementation statements matter because they explain how the real service

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:30.223
satisfies the expectation.

00:00:30.223 --> 00:00:34.665
Strong statements describe architecture, tooling, procedures, ownership, inheritance,

00:00:34.665 --> 00:00:36.480
customer responsibility, and evidence.

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:40.245
Weak statements merely repeat control language without showing how the system works.

00:00:40.245 --> 00:00:40.770
For High,

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:44.785
unsupported or generic descriptions are especially problematic because reviewers need

00:00:44.785 --> 00:00:48.980
confidence that controls operate consistently in a high-impact environment.

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:51.195
Evidence is the practical proof layer.

00:00:51.195 --> 00:00:55.520
Configuration records, logs, tickets, diagrams, scans, test results, monitoring outputs,

00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:56.200
procedures,

00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:59.804
and operational records help connect a control claim to assessment conclusions.

00:00:59.804 --> 00:01:03.800
A High baseline raises the importance of consistency between what the documentation says

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:05.580
and what the system actually does.

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:09.480
The evidence should come from real operations, not from after-the-fact storytelling.

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:12.820
Shared responsibility and inherited controls need careful handling.

00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:16.415
Some controls may come from an underlying platform, some from corporate services,

00:01:16.415 --> 00:01:19.160
some from the provider, and some from the agency customer.

00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:21.080
High does not erase that complexity.

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:23.580
It requires the team to make responsibility explicit,

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:25.880
trace inherited controls to reliable sources,

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:29.040
and explain what remains within the cloud service offering boundary.
